ter.org/image/images/big/8448"}},{"id":"7857","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5570","description":"","caption":"What you're likely to encounter on your way to the put in.","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Todd Hoffman","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7857","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7857"}},{"id":"9570","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5571","description":"This is where we were stopped by snow on the way to the put in. just past the Needle Peak turn off.","caption":"Needle Peak","poi_name":"","subject":"Todd Hoffman","author":"Kevin Pickens","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9570","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9570"}},{"id":"893188","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896847","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Mhendrickson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893188","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893188"}},{"id":"893189","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896848","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"MHendrickson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893189","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893189"}},{"id":"893190","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896849","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"MHendrickson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893190","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893190"}},{"id":"8451","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5569","description":"This correlation is based on very limited data. Please use your best judgement. The USGS gauge at Red Ives is still active although no realtime data are avaialbe. However, I am currently working with the USGS to obtain access to realtime flow data at Red Ives.","caption":"Gauge Correlation: Calder - Red Ives","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Todd Hoffman","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8451","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8451"}}],"detail":{"id":"636","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"This run is classified as a Wild River under the [National Wild and Scenic Rivers System](https://www.rivers.gov/rivers/st-joe.php) and is largely inaccessible except by trail or boaters lucky enought to catch it at the end of spring snowmelt.\n\nA trip report of this run from June 2009 can be found at [North Idaho Whitewater Boating](http://www.facebook.com/topic.php?topic=8566&uid=57788156263).\n\nThere are several nearby runs in the St. Joe watershed, including the [Skookum Canyon](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/639/) (class III/IV), and [Heller Creek](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/636/) (class IV) sections of the main St. Joe, plus the [North Fork](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/640/) (class III+), [Slate Creek](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/4153/) (class IV/V), [Marble Creek](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/575/), (class III/IV) and [Big Creek](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/4768/) (Class III/IV-). Also, access to the [Little North Fork Clearwater](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/3248/)(class IV(V)) is from Avery via Fishook Creek.","averageGradient":"60","maxGradient":null,"length":"19","correlations":[{"reachID":"636","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"levelFT","